<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="teams-and-access"
      updated="July 31, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"roles", "Roles"},
        {"inviting", "Inviting your team"},
        {"runner-scopes", "Per-member scope: runners and packs"},
        {"mfa", "MFA"},
        {"sessions", "Sessions & credentials"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="teams-and-access">
        <:dek>
          Roles control what members can do, including approvals. Runner scopes control which hosts
          they can reach. Pack scopes control which actions they can run there. API keys inherit
          their owner's access. Limiting a person also limits their agent.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/team-page.webp"
        title="emisar console · Team"
        caption="The roster — each member's role, runner scope, and a directory-sync badge — beside the account's security panel."
        alt="The desktop Team page: the member roster — each member with their role (owner, admin, billing manager, operator, viewer), their runner scope (all runners, or a specific group), and a badge on members provisioned and role-synced through SCIM — beside the Security panel with 2FA enforcement and single sign-on."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="roles">Roles</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar has four ordered roles and one specialist role. This table defines their access.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Role</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Can do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Owner</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Owners can do everything an admin can do, plus manage billing and the account.
                Only owners manage other owners. The last owner cannot be removed or demoted, so an
                account always has an owner.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Admin</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Admins manage the team, policies, runbooks, runners, pack trust, API keys, runner
                scopes, and billing. Billing includes the plan, card, and invoices. Admins can do
                everything below and everything an owner can do except manage owners.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Billing manager</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Billing managers manage the subscription, payment method, and invoices. They can
                read the member list and billing audit events. They cannot access runners,
                policies, runbooks, approvals, or actions. This role lets procurement own billing
                without touching infrastructure.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Operator</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Operators dispatch actions, <strong class="text-zinc-200">decide
                approvals</strong>, and mint their own agent keys. They can create, edit, publish, and
                discard runbooks within their runner and pack access. They can view runners, runs,
                policies, and audit records. They cannot manage teams, policies, or trust. Only
                admins can delete runbooks.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Viewer</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Viewers can read runners, runs, approvals, runbooks, policies, and audit records.
                They cannot dispatch or decide anything.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="inviting">Inviting your team</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open <a
          href={~p"/app/team/invite"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team → Invite</a>. Enter an email, choose a role, runner access, and pack access, then send
        the invitation. The one-time link is hashed at rest. Accepting it creates the membership
        with those grants. Only an owner can grant the owner role. Admins can invite members up to
        the admin level. You can suspend and restore members without losing their history.
        Suspension ends sessions immediately and blocks sign-in.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="runner-scopes">Per-member scope: runners and packs</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Roles answer <em>what</em>
        someone can do. Runner scopes answer <em>where</em>. The account creator starts with every
        runner. An invite uses the grants selected in the Invite form. SSO and SCIM use the
        provider default plus mapped directory groups. A member starts with no runner access only
        when those settings grant none. Open the member from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/team"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team</a>
        and add scopes. The member sees the union of those scopes.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            A <strong class="text-zinc-200">group scope</strong>
            follows a runner group, such as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">group: cassandra-prod</code>. New
            runners in that group enter scope automatically.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            A <strong class="text-zinc-200">runner scope</strong> grants access to one specific
            host. Use it for a contractor who needs exactly one machine.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Next, limit which packs the member can run. Each grant includes
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">all packs</strong>
        by default or an explicit list. A DBA can
        run <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">postgres.*</code>
        on database hosts
        without gaining <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">shell</code>
        access.
        Runner groups alone cannot express that limit because restricted packs often exist
        everywhere. Both dimensions apply. A member reaches only runners in their runner scope. On
        those runners, they can use only packs in their pack scope.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Scope controls current discovery, targeting, and new work. Out-of-scope runners stay out
        of fleet and dispatch surfaces, and out-of-scope packs stay out of catalog and dispatch
        surfaces. Run history and audit receipts remain account-wide. They can name runners or
        packs outside a member's current scope. An API key inherits its owner's scope. Scope the
        DBA to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">cassandra-*</code>
        and the DBA's
        agent inherits that limit. No per-key setting needs updates. Scope changes are audited.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Scope binds owners and admins too. An admin scoped to one group really does lose the rest
        of the fleet. Nobody can change their own access, but anyone who can manage them can
        change it for them.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="mfa">MFA</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        TOTP (any standard authenticator app) with one-time recovery codes — codes are shown
        once, stored hashed, and each works exactly once. Replayed TOTP codes are rejected.
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require MFA</strong>
        routes unenrolled members to setup before
        they can use the account. On Team or Enterprise, a
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >
          single sign-on
        </.link>
        session can satisfy that requirement when your IdP enforces MFA.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="sessions">Sessions &amp; credentials</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Profile lists each active session with device information. Sign out one session or
            every session except the current one with one click. Suspension and forced sign-out
            disconnect live console sessions immediately.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Magic-link sign-in uses single-use, expiring, hashed email tokens. It combines a
            browser nonce with an emailed six-character code. An intercepted email cannot sign in
            alone. Sign-in is rate-limited by IP address and email address. Failed attempts appear
            in the account audit log.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            An API key identifies its owner and records attribution. Account
            <strong>policy</strong>
            decides what the key can run. Revoke keys from <a
              href={~p"/app/agents"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >AI agents</a>. Raw secrets appear once, and emisar stores only hashes.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Keys rotate near expiry without interruption. The client installs a successor before
            switching to it. Revoking or suspending the member also revokes their keys.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
